From aa9aa3d05b8d7f283cfa2f07c648359f6da41193 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 17:18:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 401/402] hir: an array size compare reads a bound value ivar
 as its value

nalgebra 0.32.3 (librsvg 2.58.5) src/base/array_storage.rs:71, right after 0400:

    impl<T: Default, const R: usize, const C: usize> Default for ArrayStorage<T, R, C>
    where [[T; R]; C]: Default
    { fn default() -> Self { Self(Default::default()) } }

    warn: Spare Rule - [[T; Infer(0)]; Infer(1)] : Default
    BUG: src/hir_typeck/expr_cs.cpp:8923: Spare rules left after typecheck stabilised

The same happens for a plain `ArrayStorage(Default::default())` returned as
`ArrayStorage<u8, 2, 3>`: the tuple-struct constructor's path gets fresh value
ivars for R and C, the return type binds them (`Set ValIVar 0 = 2`, `.. 1 = 3`),
but the `: Default` lookup still sees `[[u8; Infer(0)]; Infer(1)]`.  Path
parameters resolve a value ivar through the placeholder callback's `get_val`;
neither array-size compare did:

- match_test_generics_fuzz (hir/type.cpp) answered Fuzzy for any `Infer` on the
  matched side.  Before 0400 the Array arm dropped the size verdict, so the first
  per-length `impl Default for [T; N]` of libcore counted as an EXACT match and
  the rule was consumed (the impl choice is irrelevant at that point, trans
  looks the impl up again).  With the verdict kept, every one of the 33 impls is
  a fuzzy candidate, "Multiple impls", and the rule never resolves.

- compare_with_placeholders (the ftic_check_params re-check of a fuzzy match)
  had the same blind spot: `[[u8; Infer(0)]; 4]` vs `[[u8; Infer(0)]; Infer(1)]`
  with Infer(1) = 3 was Fuzzy, not Unequal.

Both arms now go through resolve_array_size: a bound value ivar is its value,
a `Known` size becomes its evaluated literal so the two spellings compare equal
(a known size against a resolved `Evaluated` was an assertion TODO before);
an unbound ivar, an unevaluated expression (sha1's Resolve UFCS case) and an impl
placeholder stay Fuzzy, a rigid const param against a literal is Unequal.
match_values no longer falls through to `throw "Unreachable"` for two different
unevaluated expressions (Fuzzy, they may evaluate equal).

Assisted-By: Claude Fable 5.1
